    "available_at": "2026-11-05T17:58:20.820171+00:00",
    "body": "Packsize and Swisslog are partnering to build on-demand box making into automated warehouse systems. The companies will help customers design how goods move from storage and picking through packing and shipping , with Swisslog overseeing the integration.\nWhat’s Related\nPacksize supplies equipment that makes shipping boxes sized to each order. Swisslog designs the systems that store goods, retrieve them, and bring them to picking stations. Under the agreement, the companies will plan those steps together so customers can decide where boxes should be made and when they need to reach workers as orders move through the warehouse.\n“As fulfillment operations become increasingly automated, the inclusion of packaging automation in a holistic warehouse automation design has never been more important,” said Bill Stenger, senior vice president of sales at Swisslog. Packsize said the goal is to reduce how often workers handle orders, cut packaging waste, and help warehouses ship more orders.\nRELATED STORIES: The Power of the Perfect Box Design | Swisslog Launches FastMove Monorail System for Pallet Transport\nHow Picking and Packing Work Together\nAt a warehouse that makes boxes to order, the box can be ready by the time the items reach a picking station. Workers can then put the items straight into the box that will ship to a customer or store. That can save a transfer at the packing station, and a closer-fitting box can reduce the filler needed to keep items from shifting.\nPacksize said the companies have already worked together on multiple projects. The announcement did not name any new customers or provide pricing or a rollout schedule.\nHow Northern Tool Uses the Setup\nAt Northern Tool + Equipment’s South Carolina distribution center , Swisslog connected the retailer ’s existing Packsize equipment to an AutoStore system. Swisslog’s SynQ software coordinates the two so a custom box reaches the right workstation when the items for that order are ready to pick. The same stations handle orders headed to customers and to stores.\nAfter picking, workers seal, label, and weigh the boxes to check for errors before shipping. According to a Swisslog case study, Northern Tool previously checked each order against its invoice by hand before sealing the box.\nSwisslog said the wider automation project freed up nearly 50,000 square feet by replacing a three-level picking area. The company also reported lower freight and packing costs from making boxes to fit each order. At Northern Tool, the retailer already had the box-making equipment. The change was connecting it to picking so workers had the right carton when they needed it.",
